 orthogonal to the plane, holding the line parallel to the z-axis, as in Section 12.6. If z = ƒ(x, y) is a nonnegative continuous function over a region in the plane containing the curve C, then the graph of ƒ is a surface that lies above the plane. The cylinder cuts through this surface, forming a curve on it that lies above the curve C and follows its winding nature. The part of the cylindrical surface that lies beneath the surface curve and above the xy-plane is like a “winding wall” or “fence” standing on the curve C and orthogonal to the plane. At any point (x, y) along the curve, the height of the wall is ƒ(x, y). We show the wall in Figure 16.5, where the “top” of"},{"x":0.12944,"y":0.78531,"w":0.02763,"h":0.01076,"fs":0.01023,"n":1,"k":0,"t":"t = a"},{"x":0.15519,"y":0.79385,"w":0.02688,"h":0.01024,"fs":0.01023,"n":1,"k":0,"t":"(x, y)"},{"x":0.15392,"y":0.83091,"w":0.1059,"h":0.01853,"fs":0.01023,"n":2,"k":1,"t":"Plane curve C Δsk"},{"x":0.10061,"y":0.85454,"w":0.02763,"h":0.01076,"fs":0.01023,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"t = b"},{"x":0.04849,"y":0.87704,"w":0.20405,"h":0.01228,"fs":0.01151,"n":2,"k":1,"t":"FIGURE 16.5 The line integral"},{"x":0.04849,"y":0.89314,"w":0.24203,"h":0.04222,"fs":0.01179,"n":3,"k":1,"t":"1Cƒ ds gives the area of the portion of the cylindrical surface or “wall” beneath z = ƒ(x, y) Ú 0."}]}